<span class="cat-badge">Guide &middot; Trips</span>
<h1>Hanoi to Ha Giang by Motorbike: Route Planning | Hanoi Motorbike Guide</h1>
<p>Ha Giang is the northeast&rsquo;s serious proposition, a high province of plateaus and passes whose name is shorthand among riders for the country&rsquo;s most storied mountain roads, and it asks more of a route plan than anywhere this series has visited. The ride is a long chain of honest phases, the flat plains, the rising middle country, the high plateau roads, and a region where services thin out and the mountain&rsquo;s weather writes its own rules. This guide plans the chain without inventing distances, durations, conditions or forecasts, because the high country publishes those daily and this article refuses to plagiarize it.</p>
<p>The machine and the route briefing come from Motorbike Rental - Nguyen Tu, 112 Nguyen Van Cu in Long Bien, Hanoi, open daily 09:00 to 21:00, where the desk briefs riders daily on the northeast mountain chain. Start at the <a href="/hanoi-motorbike-trips/">Hanoi motorbike trips</a> hub, study the parent <a href="/hanoi-motorbike-trips/long-distance-motorbike-trip-planning/">long-distance motorbike trip planning</a> guide, see the machines and terms on the <a href="/hanoi-motorbike-rental/">Hanoi motorbike rental</a> page, review the <a href="/motorbike-guides/">motorbike guides</a> for riding and safety, and keep <a href="/renting-a-motorbike-in-vietnam/">renting a motorbike in Vietnam</a> open for the legal layer.</p>
<nav class="toc">
<ol>
